WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.102
Apple has recently made some huge changes
to their MacBook lineup, and a lot of you

00:00:04.102 --> 00:00:08.510
may be wondering what exactly is different
between each MacBook and which one is the

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.760
right fit for you.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.740
So today, we’ve got Apple’s complete lineup
of 2019 MacBooks right here in the office,

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.090
which should mostly stay the same into 2020.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:24.529
We’ve got the MacBook Air for $1100, the
base 13” MacBook Pro for $1300, the higher-end

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:30.200
13” Pro for $1800, and the 15” MacBook
Pro for $2400.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.050
And if you’re watching this video a couple
of months after I made it, chances are there

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:41.039
may also be a 16” MacBook Pro available,
but as of today, July 27th, 2019, it’s still

00:00:41.039 --> 00:00:42.050
only a rumor,

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.319
and it’s going to be very high-end, like
over $3,000 at the base price, so it’ll

00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:51.199
be in a league of its own, but if you’re
curious about that model, check out our channel

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:52.459
for videos on that.

00:00:52.459 --> 00:00:56.780
Now let’s get started with comparing literally
everything we can between all of these MacBooks,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:57.780
starting with portability.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.789
In terms of weight, the Air is the lightest
at just 2.75 pounds, but it’s hardly noticeable

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:05.409
compared to the 3 pound 13” Pro.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:10.369
From above, the Air is basically just as large
as the 13” Pro, so portability should be

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:14.170
pretty similar, and much more portable than
the 15”.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.619
The MacBook Pros also have more of a flat
slate shape compared to the MacBook Air, which

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:21.659
has a wedge design that gets thinner near
the front end.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:25.969
Each of these MacBooks has a high-quality
Retina resolution display, but the Air’s

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:27.090
display isn’t as nice.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.939
It’s quite a bit dimmer than the Pros, that
each have an incredible 500 nits of brightness,

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:37.090
and it’s definitely not as color accurate,
since every MacBook Pro has P3 wide-color

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:43.109
gamut, which has about 25% more colors than
100% sRGB, which is a must-have for photo

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:44.109
editors.

00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:49.689
As far as display sharpness, they all have
the same Retina-quality 227 pixels per inch,

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:53.869
and this year, every MacBook in the lineup
finally comes with True Tone technology, which

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:57.729
adjusts the white balance of the display to
match the environment around you.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:01.990
Speaker quality and volume is a huge deal
for some people, especially to me, so here’s

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:50.870
a sound comparison so you can hear the difference
for yourself.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.610
We noticed that the Air has slightly worse
speakers than the base 13” Pro, but volume,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:01.329
sound quality and bass go way up with the
$1800 13” Pro as well as the 15” model,

00:03:01.329 --> 00:03:04.930
so if speaker quality matters to you, you
should consider the higher-end models.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:10.049
However, even the Air sounds better and louder
than almost every Windows laptop we’ve tested.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:13.939
Now onto the keyboards, they’re all packing
Apple’s latest 4th generation butterfly

00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:19.079
keys that have new key switch materials which
should be more reliable than before, and we’ve

00:03:19.079 --> 00:03:23.069
yet to hear of any issues with the 4th gen
keyboard, but even if you do,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:27.480
all of these MacBooks come with a 4-year free
keyboard replacement warranty if you have

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.069
any issues with the keys.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:33.290
Apart from that, the MacBook Air’s keyboard
slopes downward towards the front edge so

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.540
it’s actually a bit more comfortable to
type on compared to the Pros.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.849
And this year, every single MacBook in the
lineup comes with a Touch ID sensor built

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:45.590
into the power button which makes it really
convenient to log in, use Apple Pay online

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.450
and install apps off the web without having
to enter your password.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.599
However, every MacBook Pro this year comes
with the Touch Bar, an OLED display that replaces

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:55.379
the top row of keys and is fully customizable.

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:59.810
However, not many people like it because it’s
more difficult to do things like adjust display

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.269
brightness, volume, and use the escape key.

00:04:02.269 --> 00:04:05.930
I personally don’t really like the Touch
Bar, but it’s not a big deal at all.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.189
And this year, every MacBook also comes with
the T2 chip, which helps with security and

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:13.599
provides hardware transcoding of HEVC video
footage.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:15.890
Now let’s talk about the ports.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:20.700
Every port on every MacBook this year supports
Thunderbolt 3, but both the 13” MacBook

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.530
Air and lower-end MacBook Pro come with only
two of these ports, compared to four ports

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.220
on the more expensive Pros.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:32.410
If you’re a basic user that doesn’t connect
to external displays or connect external devices

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.160
very often, you’ll probably be fine with
one of the two-port models,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.200
and you also have the choice of buying a Thunderbolt
3 hub just in case you do need more ports,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.690
but having four ports from the get-go is definitely
more convenient, especially for those who

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.170
use their ports often.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:51.590
Now if you love large trackpads, the 15”
Pro has the biggest one of them all, followed

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:57.150
by the 13” Pro and then the Air has a slightly
smaller trackpad, but it’s still larger

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:58.780
than most windows laptops.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.960
For battery life, the new MacBook Air is rated
at 12 hours compared to 10 hours on the MacBook

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.110
Pros, so if battery life is what you’re
after, the Air is the best option.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.710
And if you love maxing out the brightness
on your display, you could see an even bigger

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.150
difference since the Air has a dimmer display.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.150
And if you love using FaceTime, they’re
all packing 720p HD webcams, so here’s a

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.150
recorded webcam clip on each MacBook.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.140
Finally, let’s talk about performance, starting
with the processors.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:23.580
This year, the base $1300 MacBook Pro finally
gets a quad-core processor, so the MacBook

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.110
Air is now the only one in the lineup with
a dual-core CPU.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:33.290
The $1800 Pro has a quad-core and the 15”
Pro has a six-core that can actually be upgraded

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:36.850
to an 8-core if you’re willing to spend
$2800.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:41.320
Looking at raw processing power using Geekbench
4’s multi-core test, we can see that the

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.420
MacBook Air with its dual-core CPU is way
behind the MacBook Pros, and surprisingly,

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:52.280
the $1300 Pro performs almost the same as
the $1800 model, and the 15” gives you a

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.340
pretty good jump in performance as well.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.920
So we decided to look at performance per dollar
using the full retail price of each MacBook,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:04.610
and the new $1300 base MacBook Pro comes out
way ahead, with incredible amounts of raw

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.310
power for the cash, with the Air being a very
bad value, performance-wise.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:13.300
One thing that you won’t find on the spec
sheet is that the $1300 base MacBook Pro has

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:19.680
only one fan, just like on the MacBook Air,
while the more expensive $1800 and $2400 Pros

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.120
have dual fans.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.330
Something like this could definitely limit
performance for longer workloads, so we also

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:30.630
ran Cinebench R20, a more realistic benchmark
which is long enough to heat the systems up

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.870
and see how well the cooling systems work.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:37.830
And surprisingly, the single fan on the $1300
Pro didn’t really limit performance like

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.590
we thought it would, so we’re really impressed
with it.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:44.760
However, we noticed that while the dual fans
on the higher-end models were louder at first,

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.360
they eventually slowed down a little bit as
the system stabilized, but the single fan

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.460
models kept running at full blast until the
end of the test.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.740
We also tested SSD speeds, and here, we see
a pretty big difference when going to the

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:04.470
more expensive MacBook Pros, but even the
less expensive models are still fast enough

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.340
for most people, especially basic users.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:14.980
So if you’re still confused on which MacBook
you should buy, let me break it down for you.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:20.480
The $1300 base 13” MacBook Pro is by far
the best value you can get in terms of performance

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:25.510
and overall MacBook experience, but if you
don’t really need a great display or high

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:29.370
performance, and you’d rather save a couple
hundred bucks, you can go for the MacBook

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.370
Air instead.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:35.030
The Air is great if you’re a writer, a student,
or someone who just wants a premium reliable

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:40.010
laptop for doing basic computer tasks like
web browsing, working with documents and sometimes

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.380
watching some netflix,

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.810
as well as getting some extra battery life
out of it.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:46.930
For basic use, then the MacBook Air will be
the best option for you.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:51.420
Now if you’re doing any kind of professional
task like photo editing, video editing, 3d

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.650
animation or anything like that, and you love
having the best display you can get with better

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:00.250
speakers than the Air, then I would highly
recommend going for the $1300 MacBook Pro

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.250
instead.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.260
It’s the best value MacBook we’ve ever
seen.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:07.780
Now the $1800 13” MacBook Pro is for those
people who have a bit more cash left in their

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.240
budget, and are willing to spend it to get
a few more luxury improvements,

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.550
like an extra two Thunderbolt 3 ports, better
speakers, faster storage speeds, slightly

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:21.690
faster performance, and two cooling fans which
should lead to an overall quieter laptop during

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.170
heavy workloads.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:27.220
All of those things aren’t really dealbreakers,
but if they matter to you, you should consider

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.220
that model.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:33.620
It already comes with 256 gigs of storage,
so in reality, it’s $300 more than the base

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.529
model with a matching SSD.

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:39.729
And finally, the 15” MacBook Pro is for
those who know that they need the extra performance

00:08:39.729 --> 00:08:43.300
from the six-core CPU, or even upgrading to
the 8-core.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.830
And If you’re a serious 4K or RAW video
editor, this is the one to get since it has

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.250
dedicated graphics.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:53.519
And it’s also the only model that can come
with 32GB of RAM, so it’s great for high-end

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:55.100
photo editors or programmers.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:00.639
It’s for people who want the absolute best,
with the biggest trackpad, best speakers,

00:09:00.639 --> 00:09:03.470
the biggest display, and the highest performance
you can get.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:07.569
And to finish it all off, here are some general
purchasing recommendations.

00:09:07.569 --> 00:09:12.790
If you’re anything more than a basic user,
we’d recommend getting at least 256GB of

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:13.790
storage.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.269
And if you’re working with professional
tasks almost every day, we’d also recommend

00:09:17.269 --> 00:09:21.149
getting 16GB of RAM, especially if you’re
a photo editor.

00:09:21.149 --> 00:09:25.420
We also recommend that you never upgrade the
processor, unless you’re getting extra cores

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:30.810
out of the deal, which is only available on
the 15” model.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:35.970
Upgrading from an i5 to an i7 doesn’t change
anything other than a slight performance boost,

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.199
which almost always isn’t worth the price.

00:09:38.199 --> 00:09:42.889
And if you’re a very serious video editor,
get the best graphics you can afford, especially

00:09:42.889 --> 00:09:48.129
the Vega 20 graphics, and for photo editors,
graphics will do basically nothing for you,

00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:51.250
so focus on RAM first, and then the processor.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:55.610
And if the 16” MacBook Pro is out already,
check out one of those two videos to see us

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:57.470
compare it to the 15” model.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.140
If this video helped you out, tap like and
hit that circle above to subscribe.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:06.730
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one.

